<main>

<section class="section">

<div class="container">

<h2>Executive Summary</h2>

<p>

Tablet tooling documentation provides the engineering history of punches, dies, and associated components throughout their operational lifecycle. Inspection reports, maintenance records, restoration history, coating information, dimensional measurements, production assignments, and failure investigations collectively support informed engineering decisions and continuous improvement.

</p>

<p>

Comprehensive documentation improves traceability, supports quality management systems, simplifies root cause investigations, and helps ensure that tooling remains suitable for pharmaceutical manufacturing applications throughout its service life.

</p>

</div>

</section>

<section class="section">

<div class="container">

<h2>Key Takeaways</h2>

<ul class="checklist">

<li>Maintain complete lifecycle documentation for every tooling set.</li>

<li>Document inspections, maintenance, and restoration activities.</li>

<li>Record dimensional measurements and wear trends.</li>

<li>Preserve tooling traceability throughout production.</li>

<li>Support engineering decisions with documented evidence.</li>

</ul>

</div>

</section>
<section class="section section-light">

<div class="container">

<h2>Engineering Overview</h2>

<p>

Pharmaceutical tooling documentation provides a permanent engineering record for punches, dies, and associated tooling throughout their service life. Accurate documentation supports equipment reliability, preventive maintenance, traceability, engineering investigations, regulatory inspections, and long-term manufacturing improvement.

</p>

<h3>Core Documentation Elements</h3>

<ul>

<li>Tooling identification</li>

<li>Inspection records</li>

<li>Dimensional measurements</li>

<li>Surface condition reports</li>

<li>Coating history</li>

<li>Restoration records</li>

<li>Maintenance activities</li>

<li>Production assignments</li>

<li>Failure investigations</li>

<li>Replacement history</li>

<li>Storage conditions</li>

<li>Engineering approvals</li>

</ul>

</div>

</section>

<section class="section">

<div class="container">

<h2>Documentation Categories</h2>

<div class="grid">

<div class="card">

<h3>Identification Records</h3>

<p>

Each tooling set should have a unique identification number, manufacturing information, material specifications, coating details, and approved production applications.

</p>

</div>

<div class="card">

<h3>Inspection Documentation</h3>

<p>

Inspection records should include dimensional measurements, visual observations, coating evaluations, wear assessments, photographs when appropriate, and engineering conclusions.

</p>

</div>

<div class="card">

<h3>Maintenance Records</h3>

<p>

Maintenance documentation should record cleaning, polishing, restoration, recoating, repairs, lubrication, and other engineering activities performed throughout the tooling lifecycle.

</p>

</div>

<div class="card">

<h3>Failure Documentation</h3>

<p>

Failure investigations should document observed damage, probable causes, inspection findings, corrective actions, and recommendations for preventing recurrence.

</p>

</div>

<div class="card">

<h3>Production History</h3>

<p>

Production records should identify equipment used, product assignments, operating conditions, production campaigns, and significant manufacturing observations.

</p>

</div>

<div class="card">

<h3>Retirement Records</h3>

<p>

When tooling reaches the end of its service life, documentation should include retirement decisions, engineering justification, replacement information, and disposition records.

</p>

</div>

</div>

</div>

</section>

<section class="section section-light">

<div class="container">

<h2>Tooling Traceability</h2>

<p>

Traceability allows engineers to follow each tooling component throughout its operational history. Complete records simplify investigations, support maintenance planning, improve quality assurance, and provide valuable engineering data for continuous process improvement.

</p>

<ul class="checklist">

<li>Assign permanent tooling identification.</li>

<li>Record every inspection.</li>

<li>Document maintenance activities.</li>

<li>Track coating applications.</li>

<li>Monitor dimensional changes.</li>

<li>Maintain production history.</li>

<li>Document engineering approvals.</li>

<li>Preserve retirement records.</li>

</ul>

</div>

</section>

<section class="section">

<div class="container">

<h2>Documentation Workflow</h2>

<div class="grid">

<div class="card">

<h3>Receive Tooling</h3>

<p>

Create the initial tooling record with identification, specifications, coating information, supplier documentation, and acceptance inspection.

</p>

</div>

<div class="card">

<h3>Production Documentation</h3>

<p>

Update production campaigns, equipment assignments, operating observations, inspections, and maintenance performed during manufacturing.

</p>

</div>

<div class="card">

<h3>Engineering Review</h3>

<p>

Review inspection findings, wear trends, dimensional measurements, and maintenance history to determine the next engineering action.

</p>

</div>

<div class="card">

<h3>Lifecycle Updates</h3>

<p>

Continue documenting restoration, recoating, repairs, storage, replacement planning, and retirement decisions until the tooling is permanently removed from service.

</p>

</div>

</div>

</div>

