<section class="section section-light">

<div class="container">

<h2>Documentation Controls</h2>

<p>

Tooling records should be managed through controlled procedures that protect accuracy, traceability, accessibility, and revision history. Documentation controls reduce the risk of incomplete records, duplicate identities, undocumented repairs, or unclear service decisions.

</p>

<ul class="checklist">

<li>Use controlled document templates.</li>

<li>Assign unique tooling and component identifiers.</li>

<li>Record the date and responsible reviewer.</li>

<li>Maintain revision history where records are updated.</li>

<li>Use calibrated equipment for dimensional measurements.</li>

<li>Preserve photographs and supporting evidence when relevant.</li>

<li>Control access to approved engineering records.</li>

<li>Retain records according to site procedures.</li>

</ul>

</div>

</section>

<section class="section">

<div class="container">

<h2>Corrective Actions for Documentation Gaps</h2>

<p>

When documentation is missing, incomplete, or inconsistent, the response should focus on restoring traceability and preventing recurrence. Tooling should not be returned to service when its identity, condition, or engineering history cannot be verified adequately.

</p>

<ul class="checklist">

<li>Quarantine tooling with uncertain identity or status.</li>

<li>Reinspect components when prior inspection records are unavailable.</li>

<li>Reconcile duplicate or conflicting tooling records.</li>

<li>Document undocumented repairs or restoration when evidence is available.</li>

<li>Update templates that do not capture necessary engineering information.</li>

<li>Train personnel on record-completion requirements.</li>

<li>Establish review checkpoints before tooling returns to service.</li>

<li>Audit documentation quality periodically.</li>

</ul>

</div>

</section>

<section class="section section-light">

<div class="container">

<h2>Engineering Best Practices</h2>

<ul class="checklist">

<li>Maintain one controlled lifecycle record for each tooling set.</li>

<li>Use consistent terminology across inspection and maintenance records.</li>

<li>Connect tooling history with manufacturing performance.</li>

<li>Record objective measurements rather than unsupported conclusions.</li>

<li>Document the engineering basis for restoration or replacement decisions.</li>

<li>Preserve supplier certificates and coating information.</li>

<li>Review records before every major lifecycle decision.</li>

<li>Use documentation trends to improve preventive maintenance.</li>

</ul>

</div>

</section>

<section class="section">

<div class="container">

<h2>Common Documentation Mistakes</h2>

<ul>

<li>Using inconsistent tooling identification.</li>

<li>Recording maintenance without documenting the resulting condition.</li>

<li>Failing to link inspection findings with production history.</li>

<li>Omitting dimensional measurements.</li>

<li>Documenting conclusions without supporting evidence.</li>

<li>Losing restoration or coating records.</li>

<li>Keeping uncontrolled copies of engineering documents.</li>

<li>Retiring tooling without recording the engineering basis.</li>

</ul>

</div>

</section>

<section class="section section-light">

<div class="container">

<h2>Documentation Review Checklist</h2>

<ul class="checklist">

<li>Confirm tooling identity.</li>

<li>Verify product and press assignment.</li>

<li>Review inspection history.</li>

<li>Confirm dimensional measurements.</li>

<li>Review coating and restoration records.</li>

<li>Check maintenance history.</li>

<li>Review production and failure observations.</li>

<li>Confirm engineering approvals.</li>

<li>Verify the current lifecycle status.</li>

<li>Preserve the completed record.</li>

</ul>

</div>

</section>

<section class="section">

<div class="container">

<h2>Documentation Decision Workflow</h2>

<ol>

<li>Verify tooling identity and available records.</li>

<li>Review inspection, maintenance, and production history.</li>

<li>Identify missing, conflicting, or outdated information.</li>

<li>Perform additional inspection when needed.</li>

<li>Assign the current lifecycle status.</li>

<li>Document the engineering basis for the decision.</li>

<li>Complete required approval and revision controls.</li>

<li>Preserve the record for future lifecycle decisions.</li>

</ol>

</div>

</section>
