<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<div class="faq">

<details>

<summary>Why is tooling documentation important?</summary>

<p>

Complete tooling documentation improves traceability, engineering decision-making, preventive maintenance, quality assurance, and lifecycle management throughout pharmaceutical manufacturing.

</p>

</details>

<details>

<summary>What information should be documented during inspections?</summary>

<p>

Inspection records should include tooling identification, dimensional measurements, surface condition, coating status, wear observations, maintenance actions, and engineering conclusions.

</p>

</details>

<details>

<summary>How long should tooling records be maintained?</summary>

<p>

Retention periods should follow your organization's quality system, regulatory requirements, and document control procedures.

</p>

</details>

<details>

<summary>Can documentation help reduce tooling failures?</summary>

<p>

Yes. Accurate engineering records allow manufacturers to identify recurring wear trends, improve preventive maintenance, and support effective root cause investigations.

</p>

</details>

<details>

<summary>Who should review tooling documentation?</summary>

<p>

Engineering, maintenance, quality assurance, and other authorized personnel should review documentation according to established procedures and responsibilities.

</p>

</details>

